lesson: 103
title: What is left after a part is used
stars: 0
explain: When one part of a set is used or given away, the rest is still there. Find the part first, then take it away from the whole.
explain: Tom has 15 cookies and eats one third of them: one third is 15 ÷ 3 = 5, so 15 − 5 = 10 cookies are left.
vars: a=name t=thing p=3..6 n=6..60
need: n % p == 0
need: n / p >= 2
answer: n - n / p
exercise: {a} has {n} {t}. {a.He} gives away one {p.ordwords} of them. How many {t} does {a.he} have left?
exercise: There are {n} {t} in a box. {a} takes out one {p.ordwords} of them. How many {t} are still in the box?
exercise: {a} had {n} {t} and used one {p.ordwords} of them. How many {t} were not used?
exercise: One {p.ordwords} of {a}'s {n} {t} got lost. How many {t} does {a.he} still have?
exercise: {a} buys {n} {t} and gives one {p.ordwords} of them to {a.his} sister. How many {t} does {a.he} keep?
test: Of the {n} {t} on a table, one {p.ordwords} are taken away. How many {t} stay on the table?
test: How many {t} remain when one {p.ordwords} of {n} {t} is given to a friend?
test: {a} sold one {p.ordwords} of {a.his} {n} {t} at the fair. How many {t} were left unsold?
test: After {a} shares one {p.ordwords} of {n} {t} with the class, how many {t} has {a.he} got?
