exercise: How many {t} does {a} still have if {a.he} had {n} {t} and donated one {p.ordwords} of them?
exercise: In a jar there are {n.words} {t}. One {p.ordwords} of them are taken out. How many {t} are left in the jar?
exercise: {a} lives in Toronto. {a.He} collected {n} {t} over the summer, then gave one {p.ordwords} of them to a cousin. How many {t} does {a.he} have now?
exercise: {n} {t}, one {p.ordwords} given away. How many {t} are left?
exercise: One {p.ordwords} of the {n} {t} in a classroom in Dublin are used in an art lesson. How many {t} remain?
exercise: {a} had {n.words} {t}. {a.He} lost one {p.ordwords} of them on the bus. How many {t} has {a.he} got left?
exercise: {a} in Sydney has {n} {t} and leaves one {p.ordwords} of them at home. How many {t} does {a.he} take to the beach?
test: How many {t} are still with {a} after {a.he} donates one {p.ordwords} of {a.his} {n} {t} to a school in Wales?
test: A shop in Auckland had {n} {t}. It sold one {p.ordwords} of them on Saturday. How many {t} did the shop have after that?
test: {a} kept all but one {p.ordwords} of {a.his} {n.words} {t}. How many {t} did {a.he} keep?
test: From a pile of {n} {t}, {a} hands one {p.ordwords} to {a.his} teacher. How many {t} stay in the pile?
test: Take one {p.ordwords} away from {n} {t}. How many {t} are there now?
test: In Texas, {a} started the day with {n} {t}. By lunch one {p.ordwords} of them were gone. How many {t} did {a.he} have at lunch?
